WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.470
- So what made you take on something

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:03.650
emotional as this?

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.340
Like 'cause it's not
like a flipping podcast.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.840
What made you wanna really take that on?

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.580
- I like to talk to
people, and I think that,

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.990
I have a daughter with
special needs, and she's got

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.630
a very rare chromosomal abnormality,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.910
and again I feel like
in special needs world,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.127
and especially when kids
are younger, it's like,

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:21.780
"Oh we're gonna pour services into it,"

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.190
but at, in the back of
your mind, it's like,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.860
then they won't have it anymore,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.900
then they'll be normal, and
I feel like being her mom

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.010
has brought me into the
neuro-diversity and the

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:34.010
disability community where
the people are awesome,

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.849
and changing the culture, and are brave.

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:37.682
- Yeah.

00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:39.557
(crowd applauds)

00:00:39.557 --> 00:00:42.750
- And so as my dad, my
dad was a sober alcoholic

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.177
for 35 years, and he always used to say,

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.457
"Listen, do I wanna be an alcoholic?

00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:46.290
"Not really.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.187
"Am I glad for going into
this community and all

00:00:49.187 --> 00:00:51.900
"of the tools and people
and amazing journey, yes."

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.220
So it's like a little sliver lining-y,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.100
and people are super articulate about it.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.260
And I feel like it's a
conversation we all want

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.560
to be having, and I'm just
happy to be a part of it.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.840
- I think they're conversations that need

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.650
to be having, and you just never know,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.550
like all the struggles we all go through.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:10.207
- Well and I sort of
articulate it like it's the

00:01:10.207 --> 00:01:14.470
kind of goth cousin of
Oprah's "aha" moment,

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.301
it's the "oh crap!"

00:01:16.301 --> 00:01:17.779
(crowd laughs)

00:01:17.779 --> 00:01:18.612
- Yeah.

00:01:18.612 --> 00:01:19.445
- It's like it's my turn in the hot seat,

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:21.930
it's getting the call,
it's like you, that person,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.510
it's like eh.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.480
- My friend who had
like galloping leukemia

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.327
it's like you get the call, it's like,

00:01:26.327 --> 00:01:27.870
"I didn't want that call."

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.330
you know, but then it's your turn,

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:30.430
and then what happens?

